    <!-- Friend-suggestions algorithm gets its own section rather than a row
         in the API explorer — it's the one piece of real backend logic on
         the page (not just CRUD over a table), and worth spelling out. -->
    <section class="pq-section pq-algo-section">
      <h2>How friend suggestions get ranked</h2>

      <p class="pq-algo-intro">A hybrid graph and behavioral recommendation engine combines social-graph traversal (friends-of-friends) with a domain-specific signal (co-visited parks), weights the two, and falls back gracefully so new users always have suggestions, too.</p>

      <div class="pq-algo-layout">
        <div class="pq-algo-text">
          <ol class="pq-algo-steps">
            {suggestionSteps.map((step) => (
              <li class="pq-algo-step">
                <span class="pq-algo-step-num">{step.n}</span>
                <div class="pq-algo-step-body">
                  <h4 class="pq-algo-step-title">{step.title}</h4>
                  <p class="pq-algo-step-desc">{step.desc}</p>
                  {step.formula && <code class="pq-algo-formula">{step.formula}</code>}
                </div>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="pq-algo-visual" data-stage="all" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pq-sugg-graph" viewBox={`0 0 ${SUGG_SIZE} ${SUGG_SIZE}`}>
            <g class="pq-sugg-peer-edges">
              {suggestionPeerEdges.map((e) => (
                <line class="pq-sugg-peer-edge" x1={e.x1} y1={e.y1} x2={e.x2} y2={e.y2} />
              ))}
            </g>
            <g class="pq-sugg-edges">
              {suggestionSignalEdges.map((e) => (
                <line class="pq-sugg-edge" x1={e.x1} y1={e.y1} x2={e.x2} y2={e.y2} />
              ))}
            </g>
            <g class="pq-sugg-people">
              {suggestionPeople.map((p) => (
                <g
                  class="pq-sugg-node"
                  data-role={p.role}
                  data-top={p.id === suggestionCandidatesByScore[0]?.id ? "true" : undefined}
                  transform={`translate(${p.x} ${p.y})`}
                >
                  <circle
                    class="pq-sugg-dot"
                    r={SUGG_NODE_R}
                    style={p.role === "candidate" ? `--dot-r-rank:${SUGG_NODE_R + p.score}px` : undefined}
                  />
                  {p.role === "candidate" && (p.shared ?? 0) > 0 && <circle class="pq-sugg-badge" cx="10" cy="-10" r="4.5" />}
                  {p.role === "candidate" && (
                    <text class="pq-sugg-score" y="7" text-anchor="middle">{p.score}</text>
                  )}
                </g>
              ))}
            </g>
            <g class="pq-sugg-you" transform={`translate(${SUGG_CENTER} ${SUGG_CENTER})`}>
              <circle r={SUGG_YOU_R} />
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Offline mode gets its own section for the same reason the friend-
         suggestions algorithm does: it's a real design tradeoff driven by a
         concrete usage constraint, not just another CRUD surface. -->
    <section class="pq-section pq-offline-section">
      <h2>Offline mode</h2>
      <p class="pq-offline-intro">National parks are exactly where cell coverage is worst — deep canyons, mountain terrain, no towers for miles. A user who already used the app to plan a hike loses the whole point of it if they're standing at a trailhead with no bars and the app needs a live connection to show anything. Every decision below optimizes for one thing: works with zero connectivity, once downloaded.</p>

      <div class="pq-tier-grid">
        <div class="pq-tier-card">
          <span class="pq-tier-eyebrow">Tier 1 &middot; automatic</span>
          <p>Lightweight, silent, automatic. Every ordinary visit to the Parks or Map tab quietly re-saves the base park list &mdash; pins, name, About text, cover photo &mdash; to on-device storage. No user action, just a side effect of normal browsing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pq-tier-card">
          <span class="pq-tier-eyebrow">Tier 2 &middot; explicit</span>
          <p>Heavy, deliberate, user-triggered. A "Download for offline" button bulk-fetches full park detail for every park in one call &mdash; galleries, hours, fees, activities, topics, directions &mdash; and prefetches every image byte to disk. Caching a URL string isn't enough; nothing has actually fetched those bytes until something tries to render that exact URL, which doesn't happen offline.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pq-offline-callout">
        <h4>Why two separate caches, not one</h4>
        <p>If the two tiers shared a cache, the silent background refresh &mdash; which only ever has the lightweight fields &mdash; would overwrite a user's deliberately-downloaded rich data with a partial blob the next time they opened the tab. Splitting the keys was a direct fix for that clobbering bug.</p>
      </div>

      <ul class="pq-notes">
        <li>Tier 1 refreshes silently and automatically any time the network is up and that tab loads.</li>
        <li>Tier 2 only updates on an explicit tap &mdash; it's an expensive bulk operation, not something to fire on every visit.</li>
        <li>The bulk park-detail endpoint silently returns nothing if the upstream park-service key is missing or the request fails; the download handler checks for that and throws instead of caching an empty result over a good one.</li>
        <li>Cache age surfaces in the UI &mdash; "Last updated 3 days ago" &mdash; so stale data reads as stale, not as live.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="pq-offline-fallback">Network status wraps the OS-level connectivity check, treating the ambiguous state at startup as online so the app doesn't flash a false offline banner before the first real signal arrives. Screens try the live fetch first &mdash; on failure, or when auth hasn't finished loading yet (which looks identical to offline at startup), they fall through to the cached data and show an offline banner instead of an error or blank screen. The main feed gets the same treatment at a smaller scale &mdash; the last successfully-loaded page stays scrollable with no connection.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Admin dashboard — split into its own section, not a subsection of
         Built With: it's a genuinely separate surface (private, admin-only,
         not linked anywhere else on this page) and deserved its own
         heading instead of living under an h3. -->
    <section class="pq-section pq-admin-section">
      <h2>Behind the curtain</h2>
      <p class="pq-routes-sub">An admin-side dashboard allows for moderating the platform's content, creating new badges and challenges, and analyzing global trends and performance. Multiple tabs are available for different aspects of the admin experience.</p>

      <div class="pq-admin-layout">
        <div class="pq-admin-text">
          <div class="pq-route-grid pq-route-grid-admin">
            {adminTools.map((tool) => (
              <div class="pq-route-card">
                <span class="pq-route-icon" set:html={`<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">${ROUTE_ICONS[tool.icon]}</svg>`} />
                <div class="pq-route-body">
                  <h4 class="pq-route-label">{tool.label}</h4>
                  <p class="pq-route-desc">{tool.desc}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="pq-laptop-stage">
          <div class="pq-laptop">
            <div class="pq-laptop-screen-hole">
              <img src="/showcase/parkquest/web/admin-dashboard.jpg" alt="ParkQuest admin dashboard" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <div class="pq-laptop-outline" set:html={macbookOutline} />
            <svg class="pq-laptop-chrome" viewBox="0 0 3860 2540" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
              <line class="pq-hinge-line" x1="364" y1="2371" x2="3494" y2="2371"></line>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="pq-laptop-caption">Figures shown are illustrative.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="pq-admin-other">Why it exists: many aspects of the app are easier to manage from a web dashboard than from within the app. Per Apple's App Store Review Guidelines 1.2 (user-generated content), this dashboard serves as the moderation surface for user-generated content, and additionally allows for customizing the user experience and analyzing trends for optimal marketing strategy.</p>

      <div class="pq-admin-feature">
        <h4>Reconciling Apple's App Store Connect reporting</h4>
        <p>A daily Vercel Cron job merges Apple's two separate, laggy reporting pipelines into one metrics table.</p>
        <ul class="pq-notes">
          <li>Pulls two separate Apple report APIs (Sales, Analytics) &mdash; different formats, different lag &mdash; and idempotently upserts them into one table, so partial or repeat runs can't clobber good data.</li>
          <li>Auth is hand-rolled: an ES256 JWT signed with Apple's <code>.p8</code> key via Node's <code>crypto</code> directly, no JWT library &mdash; App Store Connect tokens cap at 20 minutes.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

